maps from all over the world can be  found at the click of a button but  sometimes places that look blank are  actually full of life  [Music]  this is primosh  and this is isaac  primosh and his company spatial  collective work with community  organizers like isaac to map unmapped  communities  despite the popular belief the world has  not been mapped yet  unmapped communities are literally not  found on maps  important things like roads schools and  hospitals are missing  when communities are unmapped  governments can easily overlook them  this means that they miss out on vital  resources that they should have access  to  informal settlements are usually not  connected to the former grids such as  water or sanitation or even electricity  so the maps make communities legible or  visible to the outside world  the process begins with members of the  community gathering information on foot  we use various mapping tools including  gps and mobile phones to collect data  these data points are then sent to the  team at spatial collective they plot  them and fill in the map  when citizens have information about  what they're missing they have the power  to advocate for what they deserve  madara is the second largest slum in  kenya  it's an area where there's a lot of  issues  lack of proper sanitation there's  insecurity so we've collected a lot of  data we've mapped all the hospitals  mapped all the schools  mapping empowers  me to make sure that we bring services  to our people  a picture tells a thousand words and  with map that is more than true  it can attract more investment more  interest it can help in many different  ways once you know what's there  [Music]  you
 

